  "live_records": {
    "q-110865": {
      "dom_id": "step-q110865",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [0,1].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$",
      "worked_solution": "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[2{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(0,1)=0-1=-1≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
