    "q-110988": {
      "dom_id": "step-q110988",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}},\\, & 1\\le x\\le {y}^{3}\\le 8, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$",
      "worked_solution": "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le {y}^{3}\\le 8\\}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14270355/assets/ecf0160f62333198c74110695da7f1800836f20a5d646e436df2520ddd1acfe6.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{x=1}^{x={y}^{3}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{3}-1)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(cy-\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(cy-\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{c{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{c}{y}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2c+\\frac{c}{2})-(\\frac{c}{2}+c) \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
