    "q-110984": {
      "dom_id": "step-q110984",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}cy,\\, & 0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-y, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$",
      "worked_solution": "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-y\\}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14270355/assets/2c984b3c51548a6c9181575cefe27427d12f07c499c4b44fd3fd0bddb0d710af.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}cy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}cy\\,dx]dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[cxy]_{x=y/2}^{x=2-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}(cy(2-y)-c\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}(2cy-\\frac{3c{y}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}(2cy-\\frac{3c{y}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y & ={[c{y}^{2}-\\frac{c{y}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{4/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16c}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{16c}{27}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{27}{16}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
